WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.440
Thanks to the release of The Suicide Squad,&nbsp;
the villain known as Bloodsport has taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.240
center stage in the DCEU. But&nbsp;
just who is this rampaging rascal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:16.400
and why is he such a big deal in the world of&nbsp;
comics? This is the untold truth of Bloodsport.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:17.280
"We're all gonna die."

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:18.560
"I hope so."

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.360
"Oh, for God's sake."

00:00:22.305 --> 00:00:28.240
The original Bloodsport is Robert DuBois, a madman&nbsp;
who kills dozens of innocent Metropolis citizens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:35.040
during a rampage that unfolds in Superman Vol. 2&nbsp;
#4. DuBois commands a seemingly endless arsenal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.840
of high-tech weaponry that he can teleport&nbsp;
into his grasp, including a pistol that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.680
fires slivers of Kryptonite. He is portrayed&nbsp;
as a perpetually-enraged Vietnam War veteran,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:50.640
often yelling at his victims that they're&nbsp;
wasting the freedom he fought to preserve.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.240
After Bloodsport's initial attack on a restaurant,&nbsp;
in which he massacres over two dozen civilians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.960
Superman gets involved and corners DuBois in a&nbsp;
bowling alley. Bloodsport wounds Superman with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.680
a Kryptonite bullet, but thankfully, Jimmy&nbsp;
Olsen's timely intervention saves Kal-El&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.960
from certain death. In the meantime, the fact&nbsp;
that Bloodsport has access to Kryptonite leads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:16.320
Superman to correctly deduce that DuBois'&nbsp;
sponsor is none other than Lex Luthor.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.040
It's Jimmy Olsen who figures out the key to&nbsp;
defeating Bloodsport, when his research leads him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:26.160
to the discovery that the villain never actually&nbsp;
served in Vietnam. When his number came up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:31.600
he fled to Canada, and his younger brother Mickey&nbsp;
went to Vietnam in his place, losing his arms and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:38.000
legs for his trouble. DuBois' guilt drove him&nbsp;
mad and turned him into Bloodsport. Jimmy finds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.800
Mickey and brings him to the battle, where he&nbsp;
pleads with Bloodsport to stop his rampage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.760
Bloodsport collapses in tears at the sight of&nbsp;
his brother, and the gunman is sent to a maximum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:54.160
security psychiatric facility. Of course, that's&nbsp;
not the last of Bloodsport — not by a long shot…

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:01.600
Adventures of Superman #507 opens with a heavily&nbsp;
armed masked man, who calls himself Bloodsport,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:05.920
interrupting a mugging in a Metropolis&nbsp;
alleyway. As he kills two of the three muggers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.200
you may believe he's a crime-fighter with less&nbsp;
forgiving tactics, similar to Marvel's Punisher.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.760
But when he cuts down the victim of the mugging&nbsp;
too, things start to become a little clearer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:22.240
This is Alex Trent — a white supremacist dedicated&nbsp;
to killing as many people of color as he can.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:27.360
The story bleeds into Action Comics&nbsp;
#694, and over the course of both issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.560
the new Bloodsport kills even more innocents&nbsp;
than his predecessor — mostly people of color,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:37.040
though he's also happy to turn his violent&nbsp;
attentions to whites he despises for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.600
consorting with other races. With the&nbsp;
arrival of the cyborg villain Hi-Tech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.160
it's revealed that both she and Bloodsport&nbsp;
are pawns of the demon Bloodthirst.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.840
Bloodsport is presumed dead following an explosion&nbsp;
at the end of Action Comics #694, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:58.720
following year, in Action Comics #702, he returns&nbsp;
to continue his racist rampage. Just as Jimmy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:04.240
Olsen is key to defeating the original Bloodsport,&nbsp;
it's another Daily Planet employee — reporter Ron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:09.680
Troupe — who locates Trent's Achilles' heel. Like&nbsp;
DuBois before him, Trent teleports his weapons to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:14.960
his hands, though his teleporter is implanted in&nbsp;
his chest so no one can take it from him. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:19.360
Troupe tracks down Bloodsport's weapons cache&nbsp;
and destroys it, leaving nothing for the racist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.560
to fight with. Superman captures Trent, who is&nbsp;
subsequently sent to Stryker's Island Prison.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:31.760
In 1995's Adventures of Superman #526,&nbsp;
Robert DuBois falls victim to the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:36.560
devastating villain of all: budget cuts.&nbsp;
Needing to siphon off some of its patients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.960
the psychiatric facility where DuBois is being&nbsp;
treated has transferred him to Stryker's Island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.280
Prison — the same prison Alex Trent was&nbsp;
sent to after his latest series of killings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:50.720
DuBois and Trent are destined to collide,&nbsp;
and things don't go well for either of them.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:52.720
Hoping to ease tensions in his prison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:56.960
the Stryker's Island warden agrees to a&nbsp;
boxing match between the two Bloodsports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:03.360
with the proviso that Superman act as referee. The&nbsp;
Man of Steel agrees, though he accurately deduces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.600
that allowing the boxing match probably&nbsp;
isn't a great idea in the first place.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:12.080
The match itself doesn't last very long. DuBois&nbsp;
easily dominates Trent in its early stages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:16.800
after which Trent tackles his opponent to the&nbsp;
ground. But when Superman pulls the boxers apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:20.960
Trent reveals a gun. As it turns out,&nbsp;
his Aryan Brotherhood allies have managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:25.440
to get him a new cache of weapons. A riot&nbsp;
quickly erupts, and Superman must struggle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:29.120
to control the rampaging prisoners while&nbsp;
trying to get a hold of DuBois and Trent.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:33.440
DuBois manages to get Trent's gun from him&nbsp;
and even escapes past the walls of the prison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:36.960
but he's quickly gunned down&nbsp;
and killed by the guards. Trent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:41.360
survives the riot… but not for long. His&nbsp;
Aryan Brotherhood buddies learn that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:45.600
at one point during the chaos, Superman&nbsp;
had to save Trent from DuBois. Ashamed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:50.320
that a Black man got the better of Trent, the&nbsp;
Brotherhood burns Trent to death in his cell.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:55.360
The third version of Bloodsport to appear in&nbsp;
the comics has so far proven to be the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.200
mysterious. Unlike the late&nbsp;
Robert DuBois and Alex Trent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.120
he doesn't make any claim about twisted&nbsp;
ideals serving as his inspiration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.000
and like many supervillains, he seems to don&nbsp;
his red skullcap mask purely for the money.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.480
During the late 2000s, Bloodsport appears as&nbsp;
one of five villains hired to kill the Man of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:17.360
Steel by the criminal organization Intergang&nbsp;
— the others being Riot, Silver Banshee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:21.840
Livewire, and Hellgrammite. This story&nbsp;
is part of DC's "One Year Later" event,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.480
in which Superman's powers are just beginning&nbsp;
to return after a battle with Superboy Prime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:30.160
Superman's narration indicates that&nbsp;
this Bloodsport's techniques are at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:33.680
least a little different from those of&nbsp;
his predecessors, and suggests that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:38.000
teleportation he uses to retrieve his&nbsp;
weapons is "extradimensional" in nature.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:43.040
In spite of his weakened state, Superman is&nbsp;
eventually able to defeat the five villains.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:47.600
This new Bloodsport's name is never revealed,&nbsp;
but it's clear he's more of a mercenary than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:51.680
the villains who previously took the name.&nbsp;
He expresses excitement at the money offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:56.400
for Superman's life, particularly after some of&nbsp;
his comrades have been defeated and he realizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:00.880
he could get a bigger piece of the pie. This&nbsp;
Bloodsport's given name has yet to be revealed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:05.822
and since "One Year Later," most of his&nbsp;
appearances have come in the form of cameos.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:10.160
Bloodsport made his mark on the DC&nbsp;
Animated Universe in the mid-2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:14.480
though in a relatively minor role. In&nbsp;
2005, he made his first appearance on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:19.360
Justice League Unlimited in season 2's "The Cat&nbsp;
and the Canary." The episode revolves around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:24.000
a meta-brawl run by the entrepreneurial&nbsp;
villain Roulette. In this epic showdown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.640
supervillains and heroes fight for sport&nbsp;
— some of them against their will. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:33.920
Bloodsport is introduced briefly as he's locking&nbsp;
horns with the Batman bad guy Electrocutioner.

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:38.240
It isn't clear whether or not Bloodsport&nbsp;
survives the events of Justice League Unlimited,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:42.960
though it seems unlikely. While he's usually&nbsp;
only seen in the background, Bloodsport is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:47.360
member of the Legion of Doom in the series. But&nbsp;
when the Legion of Doom's spaceship is destroyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.640
by Darkseid in the show's penultimate episode, it&nbsp;
isn't revealed whether or not Bloodsport survives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:58.400
His death is implied, however, as he isn't one of&nbsp;
the villains saved by Star Sapphire and Sinestro.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:03.280
It's never really made clear exactly who&nbsp;
this version of Bloodsport is supposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:08.080
to be. His given name isn't revealed, and&nbsp;
the unnamed version of Bloodsport had yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:12.480
to appear in DC's comics at the time the&nbsp;
series was produced. For the time being,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:16.800
this has to be chalked up as yet&nbsp;
another unsolved Bloodsport mystery.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:22.400
Before Idris Elba took the role in The Suicide&nbsp;
Squad, David St. Louis played Robert DuBois in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:27.040
the season 3 premiere of CW's Supergirl.&nbsp;
However, while he shares DuBois' name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:31.760
there are significant differences between this&nbsp;
Bloodsport and the villain from the source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:37.040
material. Instead of covering up his draft-dodging&nbsp;
like the original Bloodsport, Supergirl's version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:42.320
does have a military background — which proves&nbsp;
invaluable in his job as a terrorist-for-hire.

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:45.200
"Meet Robert DuBois. Goes&nbsp;
by the name of Bloodsport,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:47.440
which is not any sport I've ever heard of."

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:52.320
Bloodsport first locks horns with Supergirl&nbsp;
in the cold open of "Girl of Steel," when she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:56.720
joins the National City Police in pursuit of&nbsp;
DuBois and his goons. While he doesn't appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:02.320
to have any metahuman abilities, Bloodsport&nbsp;
is clearly close to peak human condition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:07.360
as he not only survives getting knocked a hundred&nbsp;
yards through the air by Supergirl and crashing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:12.400
onto the roof of a parked car, but he's even able&nbsp;
to brush himself off and escape moments later.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:17.200
It's later revealed that Bloodsport is working&nbsp;
for the crooked businessman Morgan Edge. At Edge's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:22.800
behest, Bloodsport steals some captured alien&nbsp;
technology, then uses it to cloak a submarine and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:28.320
attack the city's waterfront. Inevitably, however,&nbsp;
he's quickly defeated by Supergirl and arrested.

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:33.680
Robert DuBois' time in the afterlife&nbsp;
comes to an end in 2021's Suicide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:38.320
Squad #5. The issue opens with&nbsp;
a quick recap of DuBois' origin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:44.160
though there's no mention of his death&nbsp;
in 1995's Adventures of Superman #526.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:49.760
With all the various reboots and re-workings&nbsp;
DC's continuity has undergone since 1995,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:55.120
it can probably be assumed either that DuBois&nbsp;
was resurrected by undisclosed means in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.120 --> 00:09:00.320
intervening years, or that his death at Stryker's&nbsp;
Island Prison has been erased from history.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:05.040
Bloodsport's role in the Suicide Squad is&nbsp;
more specific than the rest of Task Force X.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:07.920
Amanda Waller sends him through&nbsp;
a number of different dimensions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:12.800
gathering intel and recruiting new captives&nbsp;
for Waller to use. To help him, Waller arms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:17.200
DuBois with a new suit and helmet to protect&nbsp;
him from the strains of interdimensional travel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:23.200
as well as a nearly unlimited arsenal of weapons&nbsp;
that he can teleport to himself across dimensions.

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:26.000
While a flashback montage early in Suicide Squad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:29.680
#5 suggests that Bloodsport has&nbsp;
been going it solo for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:34.320
he probably won't be able to manage on his&nbsp;
own for long. The issue finds him in Earth-3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:39.200
the home of the Crime Syndicate — a group of&nbsp;
super crooks made up of sadistic versions of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:44.640
Justice League heroes. By the end of the issue,&nbsp;
Bloodsport is captured by Ultraman, Earth-3's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:50.000
brutish answer to Superman, forcing Waller to&nbsp;
send in a new team to bring Bloodsport home.

